-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Monty on the Run
  Version     1.1

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Monty on the Run (1985)(Gremlin Graphics Software).z80
  MD5         0acdc707ef4cf3b4aaf94316bd0f285c

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Changes     1.1: Keeps the CPU speed you chose (F8): the sprite updates used
              to drop the Next back to 3.5 MHz

  Note        Keys: on the game's menu 1 chooses the Monty Freedom Kit, 3
              plays. Monty and the four creatures in each room are hardware
              sprites over the pixels the game still draws: the creatures in
              their own colours, Monty in white, so nobody takes on the colour
              of the cells they pass and no coloured blocks follow the
              creatures any more. The rooms, the panel and the other moving
              objects stay as the game draws them. Known issues: about 2%
              slower than the original while figures move (the sprite updates,
              measured as hook time over a recording). Where a crumbling
              platform eats into a figure, or the lift block slides one, the
              original leaves scrambled pixels and the sprite shows the figure
              whole; where two figures overlap the sprite is in front.
              Missing: the four cheats are unplayed by a person; real Next
              hardware untested.

-- MENU ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   1  Infinite lives
   2  Monsters do not kill
   3  Water does not kill
   4  No crushers

  From an archive .pok, each checked against this release's code: the lives
  counter's JR NZ made JR, the monster collision test's first byte made RET,
  the water tile range test's CP 215 made CP 0, the crusher routine's RET Z
  made RET. The archive's Infinite Shields pokes a data byte that could not be
  tied to the shields and is left out.

